  "resources": {
   "title": "Leaked a .env on GitHub? Rotate first, clean up after",
   "summary": "Once a secret is pushed, deleting the commit doesn't take it back: forks and clones keep it. Revoke and rotate the secret first, then clean the history, then stop it happening again.",
   "main_link": "https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/removing-sensitive-data-from-a-repository",
   "links": [
    {
     "label": "GitHub Docs: Removing sensitive data from a repository",
     "url": "https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/removing-sensitive-data-from-a-repository"
    },
    {
     "label": "GitHub Docs: About push protection",
     "url": "https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/secret-scanning/introduction/about-push-protection"
    },
    {
     "label": "git rm --cached",
     "url": "https://git-scm.com/docs/git-rm"
    }
   ],
   "steps": [
    {
     "title": "Revoke and rotate the secret first",
     "text": "Go to the service that issued the key and revoke it, then create a new one and keep it only in your local .env. GitHub's docs call this the first step: a revoked secret can no longer be used for access."
    },
    {
     "title": "Assume copies exist",
     "text": "Deleting or force-pushing the commit doesn't reach copies: if the commit exists in a fork, it stays accessible there, and you can't remove it from other people's clones. That's why rotating comes first."
    },
    {
     "title": "Then clean the history",
     "text": "GitHub's docs use git-filter-repo (a version with the --sensitive-data-removal flag, 2.47 or later). Rewriting history has side effects for everyone who works on the repo, so read the docs page before running it.",
     "code": "git-filter-repo --sensitive-data-removal --invert-paths --path .env"
    },
    {
     "title": "Stop it happening again",
     "text": "Add .env to .gitignore. If git already tracks the file, untrack it with git rm --cached: it removes the path from the index and leaves your local file alone.",
     "code": "echo \".env\" >> .gitignore\ngit rm --cached .env"
    }
   ],
   "notes": [
    "GitHub push protection for users is enabled by default and stops you pushing secrets to public repositories on GitHub (GitHub Docs, About push protection).",
    "GitHub Support only helps remove sensitive data when the risk can't be mitigated by rotating the credentials."
   ]
  }
 },
